1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,584
What if everything went wrong in the same month?

2
00:00:02,904 --> 00:00:07,699
The car breaks. The dog gets sick. Your laptop dies. And then a tooth starts hurting.

3
00:00:08,019 --> 00:00:12,349
Three and a half thousand dollars, in thirty days. And none of it was your fault.

4
00:00:12,669 --> 00:00:19,798
Two versions of you live through the exact same terrible month. One of them is still paying for it years later. The other is fine by Friday.

5
00:00:20,598 --> 00:00:27,923
It's an ordinary month. You've got a job, a car, a dog named Biscuit, and a budget that works if nothing weird happens.

6
00:00:28,243 --> 00:00:34,815
Rent, food, bills, a bit of fun. At the end of the month, there's usually a little left over.

7
00:00:35,135 --> 00:00:37,654
And that little bit is where the two timelines split.

8
00:00:37,974 --> 00:00:44,522
Timeline A spends the leftover. A dinner here, a gadget there. It's not much, so it doesn't feel like it matters.

9
00:00:44,842 --> 00:00:51,053
Timeline B moves fifty dollars every week into a separate savings account and calls it the bad day fund.

10
00:00:51,853 --> 00:00:56,962
Fifty dollars a week doesn't sound like much. But it's two thousand six hundred dollars a year.

11
00:00:57,282 --> 00:01:05,490
After seventy weeks, about a year and four months, timeline B has three and a half thousand dollars sitting there, doing nothing exciting.

12
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:11,987
Max thinks this is the most boring thing he's ever heard. You should invest it! Or at least buy a jet ski!

13
00:01:12,307 --> 00:01:15,383
But this money has one job. To be there on the worst day.

14
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:17,925
And then the worst month arrives.

15
00:01:18,245 --> 00:01:25,466
Monday. The car makes a noise like a shopping trolley falling down stairs. Fourteen hundred dollars to fix it, in this example.

16
00:01:25,786 --> 00:01:30,349
Wednesday. Biscuit eats something he shouldn't. Six hundred and fifty at the vet.

17
00:01:30,669 --> 00:01:36,799
Saturday. Your laptop, the one you need for work, refuses to turn on. Nine hundred for a replacement.

18
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:41,403
And the following week, a tooth. Five hundred and fifty at the dentist.

19
00:01:42,203 --> 00:01:47,869
Total damage: three thousand five hundred dollars. Example prices, but very real life.

20
00:01:48,189 --> 00:01:52,426
Same bills, both timelines. Now watch what happens next.

21
00:01:53,226 --> 00:01:56,872
Timeline A has no cushion. So everything goes on the credit card.

22
00:01:57,192 --> 00:02:05,075
The average card rate on balances that pay interest in the US was twenty one point one nine percent in August twenty twenty six.

23
00:02:05,395 --> 00:02:15,345
If timeline A pays only the minimum, the card company's formula stretches it out for over sixteen years. And adds about five thousand one hundred dollars in interest.

24
00:02:15,665 --> 00:02:20,146
Three and a half thousand dollars of bad luck becomes about eight thousand six hundred.

25
00:02:20,946 --> 00:02:27,912
Okay, let's be kinder to timeline A. Say you pay a fixed one hundred and fifty dollars a month instead of the minimum.

26
00:02:28,232 --> 00:02:33,805
Then it takes thirty one months, more than two and a half years, to pay off one bad month.

27
00:02:34,125 --> 00:02:39,733
And you pay about one thousand and fifty dollars in interest. Just for the bad luck of not having a cushion.

28
00:02:40,053 --> 00:02:45,196
And here's the cruel part. During those thirty one months, what are the chances nothing else goes wrong?

29
00:02:45,996 --> 00:02:49,955
Because life doesn't send one bad month and then politely wait.

30
00:02:50,275 --> 00:02:56,881
Six months later, the washing machine floods. It goes on the same card. Then a friend's wedding. Then a parking fine.

31
00:02:57,201 --> 00:03:03,923
The balance never quite reaches zero. And slowly, the debt stops being an emergency and becomes a permanent roommate.

32
00:03:04,243 --> 00:03:07,564
Timeline A isn't irresponsible. It's just unprotected.

33
00:03:07,884 --> 00:03:14,780
And every month of interest is money that can't go into a cushion, which makes the next surprise even more likely to land on the card.

34
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:21,013
Timeline B. Same broken car. Same sick dog. Same dead laptop. Same tooth.

35
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:27,127
B opens the bad day fund and pays all four bills. Three and a half thousand dollars, gone.

36
00:03:27,447 --> 00:03:35,358
It hurts. B doesn't enjoy it. But the total cost of this awful month is exactly three thousand five hundred dollars. Not a cent of interest.

37
00:03:35,678 --> 00:03:40,124
And the next week, the fifty dollar transfer simply starts refilling the jar.

38
00:03:40,924 --> 00:03:44,593
Side by side, the same month costs very different amounts.

39
00:03:44,913 --> 00:03:47,618
Timeline B: three thousand five hundred.

40
00:03:47,938 --> 00:03:55,438
Timeline A with fixed payments: about four thousand five hundred and fifty. Paying only the minimum: about eight thousand six hundred.

41
00:03:55,758 --> 00:04:00,147
The emergency fund didn't make B richer on paper. It made bad luck cheaper.

42
00:04:00,947 --> 00:04:02,851
And there's something the numbers can't show.

43
00:04:03,171 --> 00:04:09,835
Timeline A spends the next years with a quiet stress in the background. Every new email from the bank makes the stomach drop.

44
00:04:10,155 --> 00:04:15,147
Timeline B had a terrible month. Then it was over. Sleep is normal again by Friday.

45
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:21,261
An emergency fund isn't really about money. It's about turning a disaster into an inconvenience.

46
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:24,406
And the scariest emergency isn't a broken car. It's losing your income.

47
00:04:24,726 --> 00:04:33,689
Imagine both timelines lose their job in the same month, and it takes three months to find a new one.

48
00:04:34,009 --> 00:04:41,416
Timeline A has nothing to fall back on. Rent goes on the card, then groceries, then the phone bill. Three months can leave a debt that takes years to clear.

49
00:04:41,736 --> 00:04:48,214
Timeline B's fund won't cover three months of everything. But it covers the gap while B cuts back, looks for work, and doesn't have to panic and take the first terrible offer.

50
00:04:48,534 --> 00:04:53,155
That's why the bigger goal is months of expenses, not just one bad bill.

51
00:04:53,955 --> 00:04:57,136
Max still isn't convinced. Why keep three thousand dollars doing nothing? Invest it!

52
00:04:57,456 --> 00:05:03,006
Here's the problem. Emergencies love to arrive when markets are falling. They often have the same cause.

53
00:05:03,326 --> 00:05:10,489
In March twenty twenty, when lots of people lost hours or jobs, the S and P five hundred's average price was about nineteen percent lower than the month before.

54
00:05:10,809 --> 00:05:17,868
If your emergency money had been in stocks, you'd have had to sell at a discount, exactly when you needed it most.

55
00:05:18,188 --> 00:05:24,399
Emergency money is not for growing. It's for being there. Investing is what you do with the money after the cushion is full.

56
00:05:25,199 --> 00:05:30,494
So how big should a bad day fund be?

57
00:05:30,814 --> 00:05:32,416
Many financial planners suggest working towards three to six months of essential expenses. Rent, food, bills, transport.

58
00:05:32,736 --> 00:05:33,456
That sounds enormous, so don't start there. Start with one bad month. Even five hundred or a thousand dollars changes everything.

59
00:05:33,776 --> 00:05:40,242
Keep it somewhere separate, safe and easy to reach. Not invested in things that can drop fifty percent the week you need it.

60
00:05:41,042 --> 00:05:43,782
And how do you build it, if there's barely anything left at the end of the month?

61
00:05:44,102 --> 00:05:52,403
One. Automate it on payday, even if it's just ten or twenty dollars a week. It grows while you're not looking.

62
00:05:52,723 --> 00:05:58,238
Two. Throw windfalls at it. Tax refunds, bonuses, birthday money, the money from selling stuff you don't use.

63
00:05:58,558 --> 00:06:05,536
Three. Give it a name. People are much less likely to raid a fund called Biscuit's vet money than an account called savings.

64
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:12,106
Some disasters are too big for any jar. That's what insurance is for.

65
00:06:12,426 --> 00:06:16,606
Health, car, home or renters insurance, depending on where you live. The emergency fund covers the deductible. The insurance covers the catastrophe.

66
00:06:16,926 --> 00:06:18,702
Once a year, check what you're actually covered for. Many people find out what their policy doesn't include on the worst day of their life.

67
00:06:19,022 --> 00:06:22,679
Fund for the small disasters. Insurance for the big ones.

68
00:06:23,479 --> 00:06:26,265
And what does timeline B do once the cushion is full again?

69
00:06:26,585 --> 00:06:35,003
The fifty dollars a week keeps going, but now it flows into long term investing instead.

70
00:06:35,323 --> 00:06:40,849
That's the order that works for most people. First, a cushion. Then, expensive debt gone. Then, the money starts growing.

71
00:06:41,169 --> 00:06:47,218
Timeline A wants to skip to step three. Timeline B knows step one is what makes step three possible.

72
00:06:48,018 --> 00:06:53,823
Max, of course, hears all of this, nods seriously, and opens a savings account.

73
00:06:54,143 --> 00:06:58,102
He names it jet ski fund.

74
00:06:58,422 --> 00:07:00,175
You point out that a jet ski is not an emergency. Max says it will be, if he doesn't get one soon.

75
00:07:00,495 --> 00:07:01,203
It's a start.

76
00:07:01,523 --> 00:07:07,351
And honestly, a fund with a silly name that actually exists beats a perfect plan that never starts.

77
00:07:08,151 --> 00:07:10,938
The honest part. This is not financial advice.

78
00:07:11,258 --> 00:07:19,431
The bills in this story are example prices, card terms differ, and your own emergency fund size depends on your life, your job and your family.

79
00:07:19,751 --> 00:07:25,254
If you're already deep in debt, speaking with a non-profit credit counsellor can help you build a plan.

80
00:07:26,054 --> 00:07:31,871
Same bad month. One timeline pays for years. The other has a terrible week, and then moves on.

81
00:07:32,191 --> 00:07:36,452
Tell me in the comments: what's the worst month your wallet ever survived?

82
00:07:36,772 --> 00:07:38,478
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

